data.content:
## The problem: missing `await`

TUnit assertions are **async and must be awaited**. Without `await`, the assertion expression is created but never executed — the test method returns `Task.CompletedTask` and TUnit reports a pass.

```csharp
// ❌ Bug: assertion never runs, test always passes
Assert.That(total).IsEqualTo(42m);

// ✅ Fix: await the assertion
await Assert.That(total).IsEqualTo(42m);
```

### Fixed test

```csharp
[Test]
public async Task CalculateTotal_TwoItems_ReturnsSum()
{
    var cart = new Cart();
    cart.Add(new Item(price: 10m));
    cart.Add(new Item(price: 32m));

    var total = cart.CalculateTotal();

    await Assert.That(total).IsEqualTo(42m);
}
```

**Tip:** TUnit ships a built-in Roslyn analyzer that warns on un-awaited assertions. Make sure `TUnit.Analyzers` is referenced in your project — it will flag this at compile time so it can't slip through again.
